":"gregor_v_nyssa","s":1,"p":[83,83],"r":[34,13],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Isaiah xiii. 21. kai anapausontai ekei seirhnej, kai daimonia ekei orxhsontai , \"and ostriches shall dwell there, and satyrs shall dance there\" (R. V.). The LXX. render the Hebrew (bath-jaana) by seirhnej also in Isaiah xxxiv. 13, Isaiah xliii. 20: and in Micah i. 8: Jeremiah i. 39. Cyril of Alexandria has on the first passage, \"Birds that have a sweet note: or, according to the Jewish interpretation, the owl.\" And this is followed by the majority of commentators. Cf. Gray-"},{"id":"ebeca7a3a817","b":"ISA","v":"npnf.gregor_nyssa.refutatio_eunomii","w":"gregor_nyssa.refutatio_eunomii","a":"gregor_v_nyssa","s":1,"p":[83,83],"r":[43,20],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Isaiah xiii. 21. kai anapausontai ekei seirhnej, kai daimonia ekei orxhsontai , \"and ostriches shall dwell there, and satyrs shall dance there\" (R. V.). The LXX. render the Hebrew (bath-jaana) by seirhnej also in Isaiah xxxiv. 13, Isaiah xliii. 20: and in Micah i. 8: Jeremiah i. 39. Cyril of Alexandria has on the first passage, \"Birds that have a sweet note: or, according to the Jewish interpretation, the owl.\" And this is followed by the majority of commentators. Cf. Gray-"},{"id":"5ea0a5e677b1","b":"MIC","v":"npnf.gregor_nyssa.refutatio_eunomii","w":"gregor_nyssa.refutatio_eunomii","a":"gregor_v_nyssa","s":1,"p":[83,83],"r":[1,8],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Isaiah xiii. 21. kai anapausontai ekei seirhnej, kai daimonia ekei orxhsontai , \"and ostriches shall dwell there, and satyrs shall dance there\" (R. V.). The LXX. render the Hebrew (bath-jaana) by seirhnej also in Isaiah xxxiv. 13, Isaiah xliii. 20: and in Micah i. 8: Jeremiah i. 39. Cyril of Alexandria has on the first passage, \"Birds that have a sweet note: or, according to the Jewish interpretation, the owl.\" And this is followed by the majority of commentators. Cf. Gray-"},{"id":"714811328edf","b":"PSA","v":"npnf.gregor_nyssa.refutatio_eunomii","w":"gregor_nyssa.refutatio_eunomii","a":"gregor_v_nyssa","s":1,"p":[84,84],"r":[16,4],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"medium","cited":"Ps. xvi. 4."},{"id":"d839d1d77416","b":"ROM","v":"npnf.gregor_nyssa.refutatio_eunomii","w":"gregor_nyssa.refutatio_eunomii","a":"gregor_v_nyssa","s":1,"p":[85,85],"r":[16,25],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Rom. xvi. 25.-On Eunomius' knowledge of Scripture, see Socrates iv. 7. \"He had a very slender knowledge of the letter of Scripture: he was wholly unable to enter into the spirit of it. Yet he abounded in words, and was accustomed to repeat the same thoughts in different terms without ever arriving at a clear explanation of what he had proposed to himself. Of this his seven books on the Apostle's Epistle to the Romans, on which he expended a quantity of vain labour, is a remarkable proof.\" But see c. Eunom. II. p. 107."},{"id":"c432b6fcafed","b":"HEB","v":"npnf.gregor_nyssa.refutatio_eunomii","w":"gregor_nyssa.refutatio_eunomii","a":"gregor_v_nyssa","s":1,"p":[87,87],"r":[7,3],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Heb. vii. 3."},{"id":"d5b72b625691","b":"PSA","v":"npnf.gregor_nyssa.refutatio_eunomii","w":"gregor_nyssa.refutatio_eunomii","a":"gregor_v_nyssa","s":1,"p":[87,87],"r":[44,5],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"medium","cited":"Cf. Ps. xliv. 4, and Ps. xlviii. 14, with Ps. lxxiv. 12.","note":"Stellenangabe der Ausgabe (englische Zählung, umgerechnet)"},{"id":"d0108fc14126","b":"PSA","v":"npnf.gregor_nyssa.refutatio_eunomii","w":"gregor_nyssa.refutatio_eunomii","a":"gregor_v_nyssa","s":1,"p":[87,87],"r":[48,15],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"medium","cited":"Cf. Ps. xliv. 4, and Ps. xlviii. 14, with Ps. lxxiv. 12.","note":"Stellenangabe der Ausgabe (englische Zählung, umgerechnet)"},{"id":"c99be411266f","b":"PSA","v":"npnf.gregor_nyssa.refutatio_eunomii","w":"gregor_nyssa.ref